 "target": 45.0,
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Edna's haystacks kept catching fire. The main suspect was a sheep.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.651
  },
  {
   "text": "Three fires in three weeks, and every time, Pickles was right there, chewing.",
   "s": 3.851,
   "e": 7.421
  },
  {
   "text": "The famous Inspector Pomfrey sniffed the sheep and announced, \"Case closed!\"",
   "s": 7.621,
   "e": 11.177
  },
  {
   "text": "He locked Pickles in the chicken coop. The hens were not consulted.",
   "s": 11.297,
   "e": 14.365
  },
  {
   "text": "Pip the farmhand raised her hand. \"The hay was wet when we stacked it.\"",
   "s": 14.565,
   "e": 17.927
  },
  {
   "text": "\"Wet hay can't burn!\" said Pomfrey, and stacked a fresh wet pile as bait.",
   "s": 18.047,
   "e": 21.579
  },
  {
   "text": "By Tuesday it steamed like a kettle. By Wednesday it was toasting his sandwich.",
   "s": 21.779,
   "e": 25.32
  },
  {
   "text": "On Thursday it burst into flames, with Pickles still locked in the coop.",
   "s": 25.52,
   "e": 28.879
  },
  {
   "text": "So who lit it?",
   "s": 29.079,
   "e": 29.707
  },
  {
   "text": "Nobody. Wet hay is a buffet for billions of tiny bacteria.",
   "s": 30.007,
   "e": 33.247
  },
  {
   "text": "They eat, they make heat, and the tight stack traps it like a blanket.",
   "s": 33.647,
   "e": 36.693
  },
  {
   "text": "It cooks itself hotter and hotter, until it lights itself.",
   "s": 36.893,
   "e": 39.441
  },
  {
   "text": "Pomfrey arrested the bacteria. Pickles ate the evidence. Case closed.",
   "s": 39.641,
   "e": 43.306
  }
 ],
